usage: ./bmoe-cli -m <model.gguf> [options]

  -m, --model PATH        gguf model (required)
  -p, --prompt STR        prompt text
  -n, --n-predict N       tokens to generate (default 128)
  -t, --threads N         compute threads (default 4)
  -c, --ctx-size N        context size (default 2048)
      --ubatch N          widest graph computed at once (0 = as wide as the context).
                          Compute buffers are reserved for it, so a smaller value hands
                          RAM back to the expert cache at the cost of prefill speed;
                          decode is unaffected. Measured: a context of 2048 reserves
                          320 MiB, falling to 80 MiB at 512.
      --chatml            wrap the prompt in the model family's chat turn (gemma/chatml)
      --no-think          render the chat template with reasoning disabled
      --progress          emit machine telemetry (one JSON line per token)
      --session           keep the model loaded and serve JSON prompt requests from stdin
      --csv PATH          also write per-token metrics as CSV
      --route-trace PATH  diagnostics: write the per-step per-layer MoE routing trace
                          (which experts each layer routed, their weight, cache state).
                          Needs --moe-stream; costs speed — not for benchmark runs
      --compute-trace PATH
                          diagnostics: isolate and time EVERY graph node (per-op detail,
                          major faults per node). Serializes the graph — proportions only
      --compute-trace-layers PATH
                          same trace at layer granularity: one barrier per layer, so
                          coalescing and the expert prefetch survive and the numbers stay
                          close to an untraced run. Rows aggregate per layer (op LAYER)
      --io-trace PATH     diagnostics: one row per expert read — its (layer, expert,
                          projection), size and latency. Needs --moe-stream; this is how a
                          flash-bandwidth claim is checked against the reads that made it
      --n-expert-used N   override active MoE experts per token (top-k); lower = faster
                          but changes the output (quality). 0 = model default
  -h, --help              show this text and exit
      --version           print the engine version and exit

  Sampling (default: greedy/argmax, deterministic):
      --temp F            sampling temperature; <= 0 keeps greedy (default 0). > 0 enables
                          the chain top-k -> top-p -> temp -> dist
      --top-k N           top-k cutoff when sampling (0 disables the stage; default 40)
      --top-p F           nucleus cutoff in (0,1] when sampling (default 0.95)
      --seed N            RNG seed for sampling (default: random per run)

  Self-speculative decoding (draft a continuation, verify it in one wider decode).
  Greedy verification makes the output token-identical to plain decode; the win is reading
  the weights once per N tokens instead of N times, the risk is that N positions route
  independently and widen the per-layer expert read set. Pick one source; off by default:
      --mtp               draft with the model's own multi-token-prediction head.
                          Needs a gguf with the nextn block (Qwen3.5/3.6)
      --ngram             draft by looking the recent tokens up in the prompt and in what
                          has been generated, proposing whatever followed last time. Costs
                          no compute, no memory and no expert read, works on any model,
                          and drafts NOTHING when it has no confident match — so a step
                          without one costs exactly a plain decode
      --draft N           tokens drafted per verify batch (default 3, max 8)
      --mtp-p-min F       --mtp only: stop drafting when the head's best candidate falls
                          below this probability (0..1, default 0 = draft the full width
                          however unsure it is). Makes the draft width adaptive per step:
                          a draft not made is one fewer MTP-block pass AND one fewer
                          independently routed position in the verify batch
      --ngram-min-match N --ngram only: shortest run of matching tokens allowed to draft
                          (default 3). The confidence gate: raise it for fewer, better
                          drafts, lower it for coverage

  MoE expert streaming:
      --moe-stream        stream only the routed experts per token (MoE models)
      --cache-mb N|auto   LRU expert cache budget in MiB (0=off, or >=1500); auto=size to device
                          (default: auto whenever --moe-stream is on)
      --cache-floor-mb N  with --cache-mb auto: RAM to leave free (default 1536)
      --cache-ceil-mb N   with --cache-mb auto: upper bound on the budget (0 = no cap)
      --io-threads N      parallel expert-read lanes [1..8] (default 4)
      --cpu-mask HEX      pin the compute threadpool to these cores, strict (moe-phone)
      --io-cpu-mask HEX   pin the expert-read lanes to these cores (moe-phone)
      --ppl-batch N       with --ppl: N teacher-forced tokens per decode (prices a verify batch) (moe-phone)
      --slot-arena        expert slices in never-released pool slots read via a MUL_MAT_ID pointer hook (moe-phone)
      --defer-evict       batch evicted pages' release after each decode (moe-phone)
      --recycle-pages     move evicted experts' pages into the next miss (mremap) instead of re-faulting (moe-phone)
      --no-odirect        do not bypass the page cache for expert reads
      --dense-weights M   dense (non-expert) weight policy: mmap | warm | anon (default) | ahwb
                          (warm = page-cache them at load, best when the model fits in RAM;
                          anon = read via O_DIRECT into our own buffers and rebind, so a
                          reclaim hits zram not flash — the win on >RAM models;
                          ahwb = as anon, but into dma-buf memory the kernel may not reclaim
                          at all — not even to zram, which is what anon still pays for.
                          Android-only; measured +17.9% on a long generation, off by default)
                          Deprecated aliases kept for old scripts: --dense-odirect means
                          `--dense-weights anon`, --no-warm-dense means `--dense-weights mmap`
      --row-stream        serve dense tables the graph only GATHERS ROWS from (a token
                          embedding) from flash instead of RAM: the tensor is bound to
                          reserved address space and only the rows a token needs are
                          read. Which tables qualify comes from the graph, not from a
                          name list, so a model that also multiplies by its embedding
                          table is left alone, on any architecture
      --row-stream-mb N   resident window for those tables in MiB (default 64)
      --release-mmap      unmap the model file after load once nothing reads through it
                          (Windows: a live mapping serialises the streamer's concurrent reads;
                          needs --dense-weights anon|ahwb; measured neutral on Android)
      --load-all          debug: read ALL experts each token (A/B baseline)
      --force-cache       allow a cache-mb in the pathological band
      --overlap           overlap async expert reads with FFN compute (needs the fork)
      --repack-experts    compute streamed experts with repacked i8mm/dotprod kernels (moe-phone)
      --io-two-wave       publish a layer's first-projection reads before committing the
                          rest, so the lanes start sooner (needs --overlap and the cache;
                          experimental, off by default pending the on-device A/B)
      --prefetch K        temporally prefetch the next K layers' experts (needs the cache)
      --prefetch-sync     debug/tests only: complete each speculative read on the eval
                          thread before returning. Defeats the point (nothing overlaps) but
                          makes the integrate-then-hit path deterministic for the gates
      --drop-cold-experts F  skip a routed expert that is a cache MISS and carries less than
                          F x (1/top-k) of the routing's weight. F in (0, 1]; 1.0 is the
                          uniform share and the useful maximum. LOSSY and cache-dependent:
                          it changes the output, and not reproducibly. Off by default.
      --expert-substitute L  EXPERIMENTAL, LOSSY: before committing a decode routing, raise
                          the score of every resident expert by L x this token's score range
                          and re-rank. A resident expert wins a slot only when it was within
                          that margin of the one it displaces; weights are the router's own.
                          Runs the same NUMBER of experts, fewer of which cost a read.
                          L in [0, 1]; 0 is off. Needs the LRU cache. Measured best: 0.15
      --ppl FILE          measure teacher-forced perplexity of FILE instead of generating.
                          Every cell scores the SAME fixed token sequence, so the number is
                          a scale: comparing GENERATED text cannot price a lossy setting,
                          because greedy output only moves when a perturbation happens to
                          cross an argmax boundary, whatever its size
      --ppl-skip N        leading tokens evaluated but not scored (default 8)
      --ppl-step          score one token per decode, so a cache-dependent policy (dropping,
                          substitution) is priced in the regime where it acts. A wide batch
                          routes a layer before reading any of it, and finds almost nothing
                          resident. Slower: one decode per token
      --ppl-list FILE     score every text named in FILE (one path per line) in one session,
                          so a benchmark of many short texts loads the model once
      --ppl-choices A,B   after the text, report the log-probability of each choice's first
                          token: the multiple-choice comparison, one pass per question
      --drop-no-renorm    do not rescale the surviving weights after a drop (A/B)
      --drop-in-prefill   drop during prefill too (off: the cold cache makes it expensive)
      --route-ahead N     EXPERIMENTAL, LOSSY: commit decode routing to the prediction made
                          N layers earlier in the same forward pass (each layer's own gate
                          run on the hidden state N layers back). The router still computes
                          and gives the substituted experts their true renormalized weights;
                          a prefetch of a committed layer can then never miss. Changes the
                          output — this flag exists to measure that quality trade [0..8].
                          Excludes --predict-log / --predict-prefetch / --prefetch.
      --predict-log       diagnostics: measure how much of each layer's routing could be
                          known a layer early (the next layer's gate run on this layer's
                          input), scored against the previous-token bet --prefetch makes.
                          Changes nothing that is read; costs a barrier and a GEMV per
                          layer, so a probed run is not a benchmark run.
      --predict-prefetch  act on that prediction: speculatively read predicted expert
                          misses on the idle lanes and LRU-protect predicted residents
                          (needs the cache; excludes --prefetch). Drop-aware: experts
                          predicted below the drop threshold are not speculated.
      --predict-spec-max N  speculated predicted misses per layer [0..8] (default 2;
                          0 = retention only, the prediction spends no flash at all)
      --list-archs        print supported MoE architectures and exit

  Env overrides (flag wins): BMOE_CACHE_MB, BMOE_IO_THREADS, BMOE_PROGRESS, BMOE_OVERLAP, BMOE_PREFETCH, BMOE_N_EXPERT_USED, BMOE_PREDICT_LOG, BMOE_PREDICT_PREFETCH
